WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.190
My name is Lewis Kirvan.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.980
Thank you all for coming.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.660
So I don't have my
notes, so I'm going

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.770
to do my best because
I can't extend

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.030
the displays on this computer,
but I think I'll do OK.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.908
So this presentation.

00:00:19.908 --> 00:00:21.200
First of all, let me start off.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.210
I'm Lewis Kirvan.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.270
I'm a research manager at the
Consumer Financial Protection

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:26.410
Bureau.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.770
And I'm supposed to say this,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.840
I am not a representative
of the US government.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.110
I am just myself right now.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.880
I don't know if you
can tell, but I'm not.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.080
The point of this talk is to
help data analysts with words,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.840
and I think words
are actually super

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.550
important for data analysts.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.290
If you've ever communicated with
a leader in your organization,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.830
you'll know that a lot of
communication about data

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:55.810
actually flows through words
and that getting good at words

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.590
is really going to differentiate
you as a data person.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.170
So the first part
of this talk is

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.750
about identifying
situations when you're going

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.400
to need more words, right?

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.270
So that's part one.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.260
Part two is how to make words a
little bit easier to work with.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.348
So this is focusing
on a couple of things,

00:01:14.348 --> 00:01:16.390
but also when you want to
actually generate text,

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.400
it's going to give you
some tips, a few tips

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.530
to help you generate text.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.810
So that's the
structure of this talk.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.770
Let's get rolling.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:25.380
All right.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.910
So, chapter one, when
do you need more words?

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.200
So this is probably the
most familiar diagram

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.120
at the conference.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.300
Everyone's seen this
like way too many times.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.060
It's kind of funny
that I put it in here,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.280
but this document
really captures

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.000
a lot about the data
products process, the process

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.330
of creating data products.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.140
But I think it leaves out
one really important thing.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.100
You're actually in the
middle of that cycle, right?

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.540
And you're doing stuff. Like,
there you are, hands on hips.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.040
So you're in the
middle of the cycle

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.250
and you're actually doing
stuff and gaining context

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.922
as that cycle sort
of spins around.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.880
Right? you're actually
learning about the data,

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.050
learning about the
analysis, learning

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.430
about what the goals are.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.620
So you might do some
stuff like this.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.240
You might look at
the application that

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.020
generates the data, you might
read a survey instrument,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.870
you might talk to
application users,

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.430
you might read the
data documentation,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:25.040
if you're extremely lucky. You
might read relevant research,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.050
or talk to experts that know
about how and why the data was

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:31.010
generated.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.090
In my opinion, that's all
statistics and data science

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:34.590
too.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.080
That's all important stuff.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.060
And knowing about the
context of your data

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.680
is how you don't make
really stupid errors.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.560
So it's super important
and it's something

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.420
that I think we don't
emphasize enough, actually.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:46.920
All right.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.570
So you did your analysis.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.670
You got all this context.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.440
And it's time to communicate.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.550
This is the fun part.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:58.610
R is really good
at this. Ugh, right?

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.925
I don't want to knock
on dashboards too much.

00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:03.050
You know, I've built a lot
of dashboards, actually,

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.520
in my life.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.070
And, you know, this
dashboard actually

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:08.220
might be fine for someone.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.750
It's just what I got when I
Googled the busiest dashboard

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.520
or something like that.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.740
So, you know, I think
that dashboards are fine,

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.580
but when you reach for a
communication tool, you know,

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.530
you want to think a
little bit before you

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.960
grab something that's
very visually dense

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.870
or has a lot of moving parts.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.810
So before you
communicate, you want

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.942
to think about your audience.

00:03:31.942 --> 00:03:34.400
And so the rest of this talk
or the rest of this first part

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.567
is going to be a little bit of

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:39.280
how about how you think
about your audience.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.530
You want to bring people
along on your journey.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:48.880
So, you know, people
need to be brought along.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.300
And a great first question
to ask is, how much of that

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.460
context I built up
during my analysis

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.380
does my audience share?

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.240
You know, if they don't
share the context,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.170
it's going to be a
difficult journey, right?

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.050
It's like, how much
do you need to pack

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.240
to get that person to
the destination you

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.880
want them to go to?

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:08.380
All right.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.755
So I have a couple
of limit cases

00:04:09.755 --> 00:04:12.780
here, examples from my own work.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.510
So earlier this year, I
wrote a report to Congress.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.250
It's required by law, and
it's about credit reporting,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.470
consumer complaints.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.390
It's got six charts in it.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.390
Two tables.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.560
Yeah OK, cool.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.010
It started in R Markdown and
it's got 50 pages of context.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:30.510
All right.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.400
Congress is one limit case.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.740
Congress needs,
like, a lot of stuff.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.590
They need detailed explanations
of all the fields and all

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.980
the key terms.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.230
They need to know about the
data collection process.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.740
And they need to know
about, like the law that

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.950
governs the data collection
and all that context.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.640
I actually have a JD, so like
that part helped me here,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.900
but Congress
needs a lot of stuff.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.630
So that's like one limit case.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.360
Another limit case is your
immediate team, right?

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.180
So a ways back, I made this
dashboard, took me a day.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.990
It's got three charts
in it, six KPIs, less

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.260
than paragraph of text.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.060
Beautiful, right? Just me and my
team tracking something.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.080
The labels are kind of terse.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.440
I don't really describe the
KPIs because there our KPIs,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.760
we know what they are.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.560
And you know, like the data
elements are just named what

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.810
they are in the system, right?

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.795
So this is the other extreme.

00:05:23.795 --> 00:05:26.170
You're likely going to be
somewhere in the middle, right?

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:27.750
But these are the
two limit cases.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.870
So I have another
example that I think.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.210
So just to kind of
sum up that idea,

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.740
less shared context
means more context,

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.240
words, are going to be
in your data product.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.978
More shared contexts means
you can include less context

00:05:42.978 --> 00:05:44.520
in your product,
but you should still

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.770
document it because
you might forget

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.228
other people might work on it.

00:05:48.228 --> 00:05:49.270
So it's kind of a seesaw

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.290
there. You can go,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.170
does it end up in
the documentation

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.690
or does it end up
in the data product?

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.850
So here's another example.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:00.937
Moving from an internal set of
tools to something external.

00:06:00.937 --> 00:06:03.270
I like this example because
it's the same data presented

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.940
to two different audiences.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.170
So the internal tool is an
interactive Tableau dashboard.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:14.000
It's got a lot of
charts, dozens of charts.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.570
You know, Tableau
is good for that.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.540
And then the other
example, the field names

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.550
correspond to the system names.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.860
It's kind of a typical KPI
type dashboard for your team.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.200
But when this team went to
report out on this stuff,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.900
you know, they started with
this and they kind of just

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.740
started to repurpose it
for external communication.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.440
But we were going to actually
share this with banks

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.960
and we're sort of assessing
their performance.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.090
And I knew, when I
got sort of thrown

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.637
into helping them, that this
document was meant to travel.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:46.470
It wasn't just going
to go to the person who

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:47.980
works on this stuff every day.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.440
They were going to give
it to their supervisor

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.520
and maybe their supervisor
would give it to someone else.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.950
And it probably won't go
to the board of directors,

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.460
but it can go travel.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.570
And so the context had to
travel with the document.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.860
So we have this much
simpler version.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.560
So there's only three
simple static charts.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.130
One kind of gives you a time
series of your performance,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.310
one gives you your
average or each one

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:11.190
gives you your average, and then
compares you against your peers

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.140
and against some sort
of overall performance.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.170
So that's pretty nice.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.450
Some of the charts from
the internal dashboard

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.150
are actually sentences
and paragraphs now.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:26.110
So the names are all also
defined in an introduction.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.680
You know, a sentence
is actually if you just

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.690
like tell someone
what the chart means,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.560
that's actually denser
than a chart, right?

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.750
So in some places where
you want to do that stuff.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:41.052
I also included different
text depending on thresholds

00:07:41.052 --> 00:07:42.010
and other stuff, right?

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:45.060
So some of these
sentences and paragraphs

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.070
didn't really apply to
some of these companies.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.982
And so we threshold
at it and they just

00:07:49.982 --> 00:07:51.190
don't appear in the document.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.130
So it doesn't like bloat
and bloat and bloat.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:58.230
So that's an example of moving
from internal to external.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.440
Summarize this with
some key factors.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.830
The biggest one is
external, right?

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.380
If they're outside
your org, they

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.220
don't have any of
your context likely.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.460
But even within your
org, you can think

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.860
about levels of externality,
right? There's your immediate team,

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.200
and then there's
concentric circles.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.410
And as you kind move
further away from your team,

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:18.990
you need to be adding
more context in.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.520
Another key factor
is if you're kind

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.420
of dealing with a less
technical audience.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.177
Less technical audiences
mean you can't use shortcuts

00:08:27.177 --> 00:08:29.010
that you might use when
you're communicating

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.117
with a technical audience.

00:08:30.117 --> 00:08:31.950
Like you're not just
going to present an ROC

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.630
curve to your
executive and say, hey,

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.130
look, my model is
really good, look,

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.470
because they're not
going to get that right.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.900
So you have to think
about adding more context

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.669
in for those folks.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.310
Decision makers are important
because they actually

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.770
need context to trust
what you're telling them.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.190
If they don't have
appropriate context,

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:50.940
they're not going to
trust you and they're

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.138
going to reject your findings
even if you're right.

00:08:53.138 --> 00:08:54.930
Right? You can pound
the table all you want,

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.890
but they need enough
context to trust you.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:58.680
And I've had this happen to me.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.990
It's pretty disappointing,
honestly, when you're like,

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:03.268
look, it works, it's great.

00:09:03.268 --> 00:09:05.560
And they're like, I don't
know what this is even. Right?

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.860
So you have to give them
that appropriate context.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.920
Time crunched is actually kind
of counterintuitive in a way,

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:11.907
right?

00:09:11.907 --> 00:09:12.490
Time crunched

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.180
people you want to give
them as little as possible

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.430
in some ways, but
you're only going

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:18.880
to get them one time, right?

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.280
So those time crunched
people have one shot.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.640
And if they don't walk away
with the context that's

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.622
necessary to understand
this, they'll

00:09:25.622 --> 00:09:27.330
either misinterpret
it, not interpret it,

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:28.270
or forget about it.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.750
Right? So those time crunched
people are important too.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.090
So those are some
key factors when

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:36.610
thinking about this question
of like, what should I build?

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:40.710
How much context do I need
to put in it for my audience?

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.270
All right.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:46.980
Chapter two, how to make
words a little bit easier.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.280
All right, I'm done, guys.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:49.560
That's it.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.450
Actually, now I'm done.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:53.950
Not really.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.820
I actually have some tips.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.400
So my tips are just three
kind of simple things.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:07.560
Use some templates, use design
resources, and then partner up.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.780
All right, let's start
with the first one.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.620
So the context here is
you're generating text.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:17.420
If you've ever generated a
document with a lot of text.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.200
What happens is your
environment just

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.000
gets littered with variables.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.275
There's all kinds of
different variables.

00:10:23.275 --> 00:10:24.900
Some of these documents
I put together,

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.910
you know, ten, 15
pages, you know,

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:28.820
you've computed 10
different means.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:30.070
They all have different names.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:34.330
And when you make a mistake,
debugging is annoying and hard.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:36.900
And so what you can
do is actually add

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.030
a language engine
that will allow

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.910
you to put templates in
your R Markdown document

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.320
and then fill them with data.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.380
This is a really simple
language, really simple

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:48.880
function.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:50.970
You can just add at the
top of your Markdown file,

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.960
it's a knitr engine, here,
and this works in quarto.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.080
I just did it right.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.570
That works.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.340
So you add this, this
is using whisker,

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.450
which uses
mustache templates.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.140
I'll talk about that
in just one second.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.380
Options code.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:08.550
That's just the stuff
that's in your code block.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.710
So you put your template
in there and then,

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.380
as an argument, you
supply some data,

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:18.198
and then it will render and
make something nice for you.

00:11:18.198 --> 00:11:19.490
You could do it with glue, too.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:20.613
So here it is with glue.

00:11:20.613 --> 00:11:21.780
Basically the same function.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.988
You need more stuff for glue.

00:11:22.988 --> 00:11:25.160
This would be quite a bit
longer if I were to really

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.300
put this together for glue.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.630
But just so you can see,
the basics are the same.

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.320
This still works, but
there's stuff you want to do.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:33.980
All right.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:36.770
So here's an example
of just that concept

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:41.540
of taking your namespace or
using a list as a namespace

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:45.540
so that you don't basically end
up cluttering your namespace.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.510
So I'm generating
one sentence here

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.445
and there's one, two, three,
four, five or so six variables.

00:11:51.445 --> 00:11:53.570
There's a bunch of variables
just for one sentence.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:54.140
Right?

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.870
and what I like to do is
when I have a document

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.600
with some structure in it, like
a section, I'll have a list

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.820
and I'll pack all the variables
for that section into the list

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:03.960
that's named the same.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.660
And then my environment
stays like relatively pretty.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:09.410
Debugging gets easier,
and my mental load

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:13.980
as a developer doesn't sort of
go haywire completely, right?

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:15.650
And so then when
you go to do this,

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:17.540
it's actually pretty simple.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.240
You have right here, this
is a mustache template,

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:21.920
a really simple one.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:22.830
Mustache is great.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:24.990
It has some other it's
a logicless template.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.260
So it's very-- it's almost
like Markdown in a way,

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.890
but you can add some
basically data dependent stuff

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.710
that will make stuff
appear or not appear.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:36.820
There's a few other nice little
features, but it kind of just

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:37.320
works.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.970
So there's the
data filling it in.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:41.718
The sentence now exists
and there's a glue version.

00:12:41.718 --> 00:12:43.760
You need some post-processing
on glue to make it

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:45.020
not have quotes and stuff.

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:46.790
The real function
I use is longer,

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:49.400
but I didn't want to go
into all that detail.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.670
So that's tip one.

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.730
I think it's pretty helpful.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:56.030
And you know, if you do more
of this stuff, especially

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.510
when you're generating a lot
of text, it's super helpful.

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:01.960
Tip two, use existing
design resources.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:07.510
Design is super duper important
when you're making a document

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:08.810
that you want people to read.

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:11.350
You need consistency
and you need signposting

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.900
to make the document useful.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.400
And I am kind of took a weird
path to being a researcher

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.400
and data analyst.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.670
I've got a degree in African
history and a JD.

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.730
So I spent like 30
years trying to make

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:26.890
other kinds of good documents.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.860
So some of this stuff
is natural to me,

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:32.920
but I think it should be
more natural to data analysts

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:33.740
as well.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.070
So someone at your
org has probably

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.250
thought about those issues, that
signposting, that consistency,

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.980
and they have resources for
you, so you should steal them

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:43.780
basically.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:47.630
So it's almost as
easy as this, right?

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:52.930
So you have in your
YAML a reference stock.

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:55.270
The actual package comes
with a reference doc.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:58.120
You look at that reference,
it shows you all the styles

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:05.530
that you need and actually quarto has
some surprisingly good actually

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:08.260
documentation on
where it styles if you

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.030
need some documentation on it.

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.160
And so then you can
either adjust the styles

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.500
yourself or you can
actually copy and paste

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.720
styles between documents.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.570
So if you have a nice
reference document,

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:22.360
you can go ahead and use that.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.090
Great. So this is what it
looks like when you use it.

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:29.930
It's just like any other pandoc
kind of colon braces thing.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.640
You put it in there and
then the style gets applied.

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:40.303
This is actually a style from
my org's work DOCX template

00:14:40.303 --> 00:14:42.470
that we use or one of our
DOCX templates that we use.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:45.020
And I have no idea how to make
that pull quote in Word.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:45.980
I don't want to know.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:47.950
That sounds like
a pain in my butt.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:51.778
So instead I use I
leverage someone else who

00:14:51.778 --> 00:14:52.820
did know how to use that.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:56.590
And in my documents I
can use this feature.

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:57.962
All right.

00:14:57.962 --> 00:15:00.170
This is cheating because
it's not really about words.

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:02.545
But it saved me so much time
over the course of my career

00:15:02.545 --> 00:15:03.940
that I just have to say it.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:06.190
You should if you don't have
one of these at your org,

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:09.550
a minimal, minimal package to
package up your design system

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.620
is super helpful.

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.110
So you can put all your
organization's colors

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:15.543
into an easy to access list.

00:15:15.543 --> 00:15:17.210
This is going to save
you all that time.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.250
Looking up hexes and
remembering hexes.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:22.450
Amazing. And then
just a minimal ggplot theme

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.018
that gets you most of the way.

00:15:24.018 --> 00:15:25.810
If you have one of
these at your org, like,

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:27.500
people will love you.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.380
You know, I maintain
the one at my org now

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:30.773
and people love
me just for that.

00:15:30.773 --> 00:15:31.940
And I didn't even create it.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.530
So this is like
something you should do.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:34.510
And it'll save you
enough time that you

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:37.090
can spend more time focusing
on writing and polishing

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:38.470
your writing.

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:39.760
All right.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:43.210
My tip number 3 is sort
of a general life tip.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:46.540
Ask for help and
partner up when you can.

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.670
It really applies
a lot with writing.

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.240
Writing is really hard.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:52.510
Writing is really hard
for analytical people

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:55.100
because you have high
expectations for your audience.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.740
You shouldn't have high
expectations for your audience.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.677
And a writer can help
you with that, right?

00:15:59.677 --> 00:16:01.010
They can help you lots of stuff.

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:02.302
They can help you with grammar.

00:16:02.302 --> 00:16:05.440
The grammar for language.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:05.940
Oh, good.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:06.680
One, one, one

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.010
laugh for my dad joke.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.190
Anyways, they can give
you an outside opinion,

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:14.710
like, does this make
any sense at all?

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.880
And they can help you
think about your audience.

